\subsection{Detection Signal Failure Modes}
\label{sec:signal_failure}

\paragraph{Min-K\%++ directional reversal.}
Min-K\%++ \citep{zhang_2024_minkpp} exhibits a \textit{directional reversal} at the 0.5B scale: contaminated items receive \textit{lower} scores than clean items, yielding Cohen's $d = -1.55$ at d005 and $d = -1.98$ at d100 relative to d000. A practitioner applying Min-K\%++ at this scale would conclude that contaminated items are \textit{less} likely to be memorized. At 1.5B, discrimination turns mildly positive ($d \approx +0.3$); at 3B, moderately positive ($d \approx +0.7$). We hypothesize that SFT injection at 0.5B disrupts the token-level surprisal landscape so severely that contaminated items (overfit at the response level) paradoxically show reduced token-level fluency.

\paragraph{Signal reliability across scales.}
Figure~\ref{fig:signal_heatmap} summarizes detection AUC across signal-scale-dosage combinations. S1 (embedding similarity) achieves AUC $> 0.95$ at d005 and above for all scales. S4 (Self-Critique) performs near-random at 0.5B and reaches marginal discrimination at 1.5B+. RRF fusion degrades to S1-only performance because S2 and S4 contribute noise at small scales.

Cross-family analysis with Llama-3.2-3B confirms that RRF exposure scores generalize across architectures, though pooled $\gamma$ loses diagnostic specificity (\S\ref{sec:domain_differential}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_signal_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Detection signal AUC across scale, dosage, and signal type. S1 (embedding similarity) achieves AUC $>0.95$ at all scales; S2 (Min-K\%++) reverses at 0.5B; S4 (Self-Critique) is near-random below 1.5B.}
\label{fig:signal_heatmap}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Identifiability Boundaries}
\label{sec:identifiability}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_identifiability_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{$\gamma$ recovery as a function of signal AUC and ensemble size $J$. Bias $<0.05$ and 95\% CI coverage $\geq90\%$ require $J\geq50$ at AUC$=$0.55, but only $J\geq16$ at AUC$\geq$0.90.}
\label{fig:identifiability_heatmap}
\end{figure}

The simulation study (20 conditions, 10 seeds each, 200 runs) establishes the operating envelope. Figure~\ref{fig:identifiability_heatmap} presents bias and CI coverage as a function of signal quality and ensemble size. Recovery succeeds (bias $< 0.05$, 95\% CI coverage $\geq 90\%$) at AUC as low as 0.55 with $J \geq 50$. Posterior CI width scales as $J^{-0.48}$, close to the parametric $J^{-0.5}$ rate. Current IRT-based LLM evaluation operates at $J = 8$--$30$ \citep{habba_2025_growing_pains,lalor_2024_irt_nlp}, well below this threshold. Signal quality is the binding constraint: when AUC $\geq 0.90$ (as S1 achieves), even $J = 16$ suffices. Our $J = 13$ results confirm the analysis: calibration via $\theta$ works, but per-domain $\gamma$ remains exploratory.

\subsection{Exploratory Domain Analysis}
\label{sec:domain_differential}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_domain_gamma.pdf}
\caption{Per-domain $\gamma$ estimates with 95\% CIs ($J$=13 Qwen2.5). Within this family, STEM domains show positive $\gamma$ while Humanities and Social Sciences are negative. Only Social Sciences reaches significance. These patterns are family-specific (\S\ref{sec:cross_family_validation}).}
\label{fig:domain_gamma}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Within-family directional heterogeneity.}
Table~\ref{tab:domain_gamma} and Figure~\ref{fig:domain_gamma} present per-domain $\gamma$ estimates ($J=13$ Qwen2.5, 1.5B and 3B; 0.5B excluded per \S\ref{sec:injection}). Within the Qwen family, STEM domains show positive $\gamma$ (Math $+0.50$, Science $+0.50$, CS/Eng $+0.50$) while Humanities ($-0.31$) and Social Sciences ($-0.57$) are negative. Only Social Sciences reaches significance ($[-1.05, -0.10]$), consistent with SFT injection on factual QA pairs disrupting contextual reasoning. ARC-Challenge shows a large negative $\gamma$ ($-1.46$, CI excluding zero), reinforcing this pattern: science reasoning items are similarly vulnerable to capability disruption from factual SFT injection. However, ARC model fit is marginal (PPP $= 0.03$); this significance should be interpreted cautiously. The pooled MMLU estimate ($\gamma_{\text{pooled}} = +0.11$, CI $[-0.40, +0.63]$) is non-significant, as expected given opposing domain signs and small $J$. These directional patterns are family-specific: cross-family pooling shifts domain-level $\gamma$ estimates, and the STEM-positive pattern observed within Qwen does not generalize across families (\S\ref{sec:cross_family_validation}).

Dose-response analysis (Figure~\ref{fig:dose_response}) shows scale-dependent effects: at 1.5B and 3B, accuracy increases with dosage, while at 0.5B, accuracy \textit{decreases} (0.41 $\to$ 0.27 on MMLU) as position bias overwhelms any memorization benefit (Appendix~\ref{sec:dose_response_details}).

\subsection{Cross-Family Validation}
\label{sec:cross_family_validation}

\paragraph{Calibration generalizes; diagnosis does not.}
To test whether these properties extend beyond a single architecture, we pool Qwen2.5 and Llama-3.2-3B in a single IRT run (concurrent calibration, placing all $\theta_j$ on a common scale; d000 models serve as anchors; Table~\ref{tab:cross_family}). Calibrated MAE remains below 0.02 on every benchmark (MMLU 0.008, GSM8K 0.008, ARC 0.015), confirming that IRT ability estimation generalizes across model families. All three $\gamma$ estimates are non-significant, with CIs spanning zero. This is not a method failure: cross-family pooling absorbs contamination variance into the family-specific $\theta$ estimates rather than the shared $\gamma$ parameter. PPP values indicate acceptable model fit at the benchmark level (MMLU 0.60, GSM8K 0.83, ARC 0.45). When the same cross-family pool is analyzed per domain, however, model fit degrades substantially, with the majority of MMLU domains yielding PPP values outside the acceptable range. This confirms that a single shared $\gamma$ cannot capture domain-level contamination effects across architectures with different susceptibility profiles, providing additional evidence that $\gamma$ diagnosis requires family-stratified analysis.

\paragraph{$\theta$ asymmetry across families.}
The GSM8K results reveal that different families respond to contamination through $\theta$ rather than $\gamma$. Qwen2.5-3B shows a large ability shift from d000 ($\theta = 0.15$) to d100 ($\theta = 0.72$, $\Delta\theta = +0.57$), while Llama-3.2-3B shows minimal change ($\Delta\theta \approx +0.05$). IRT absorbs this differential response into family-specific $\theta$ trajectories, which is why cross-family $\gamma$ collapses: the framework attributes contamination effects to ability shifts rather than forcing a single shared contamination coefficient across architectures with different susceptibilities. This case illustrates the decomposition in action: when contamination susceptibility varies across architectures, IRT captures these differences through $\theta$ estimation, maintaining calibration without requiring an identifiable shared $\gamma$.

